USAGE:
1) templates for the input files can be obtained with the command
   'secdec -g --prep'

2) Set the desired parameters in param.input and define the integrand function in math.m
    you can also rename param.input into [myparamfilename] and math.m into [mymathfilename],
    and move them to [myworkingdirectory] (recommended)
    Then use 'secdec -g -d myworkingdirectory -p myparamfilename -m mymathfilename'


3) a) if the calculations are done sequentially: (no batch system used)
    
    The results are saved to the files integralname/integralname_[point]epstothe*.res,
     integralname/integralname_[point]full.res. 
    The result file can also be automatically displayed with your specified text editor, 
    if the editor is set in param.input


3) b) if using a batch system on a cluster: 
     
     i) default batch setup (PBS): 
        
	When all jobs have finished execute the command 
	'secdec -d myworkingdirectory -p myparamfilename --collectresults'. 

     ii) customised setup:
         
        manually edit perlsrc/makejob.pm and perlsrc/launchjob.pm (required) to be compatible with
	your batch system syntax.
	To ensure the maximum number of jobs allowed in the queue is not exceeded, 
	manually edit perlsrc/makesub.pm
	(optional - if not done, max jobs allowed = infinity).
	This writes the desired syntax to the scripts subdir/integralname/job[polestructure], which are
	launched automatically by subexp.pl (or finishnumerics.pl).
	The submission files for numerical integration are created at
	subdir/integralname/polestructure/epstothe[o]/polestructure.[o].[ni] or
        subdir/integralname/polestructure/epstothe[o]/[nv]/polestructure.[o].[nv].[ni],
	where [o] is the order of epsilon, [nv] is the number of integration variables (only when IBP used)
	and [ni] labels which executable (intfile[ni].exe) is to be run.
	these are launched by subfile.pl in the same directory, which is run automatically by
	perlsrc/polenumerics.pl, or perlsrc/finishpolenumerics.pl
	
        When all jobs have finished type  
	'secdec -d myworkingdirectory -p myparamfilename --collectresults'. 

4) To remove intermediate auxiliary files after completion of the calculation, 
     you can use the command './makeclean'.

  called with no arguments this removes intermediate perl scripts, makefiles, .o files, *sf*.f, executables,
  and where applicable job submission files, but leaves the fortran files created by Mathematica, 
  so that different  numerical points can be calculated without rerunning the Mathematica code.
  called with the argument 'all' (i.e. "./makeclean all"),  it removes everything except
  the [integralname]*full.res file.
  The executable cleanall.sh will remove all job error/output files from the batch
  system in the "general/" directory.


  USEFUL ADDITIONAL COMMANDS:  
  './finishnumerics.pl [-d myworkingdirectory -p myparamfilename]': 
   if the 'exe' flag in 'param.input' resp. 'myparamfilename' is set < 4, this 
   will complete the calculation without redoing previous steps. 
   Then execute the command'./results.pl [-d myworkingdir -p myparamfilename]'
   to collect and display the result as above.
  
  './justnumerics.pl [-d myworkingdirectory -p myparamfilename]': 
   to produce results for a different kinematic point: (if fortran files f*.f have been produced already)
   change the point name and the numerical values for the masses/invariants in the parameter file
   and then execute the command 
   './justnumerics.pl [-d myworkingdirectory -p myparamfilename]' to produce numerical results 
   for different points in phase space 
   (it skips the mathematica subtraction+epsilon expansion step which can be done once and for all,
   as the kinematic variables at this stage are still symbolic).
   Then type './results.pl [-d myworkingdirectory -p myparamfilename]'
   to collect and display the result as above.
  
  './createdummyfortran.pl [-d myworkingdirectory -p myparamfilename]':
   when implicit functions have been used at the algebraic stage, 
   this command produces optimized fortran code to be used at the numerical integration stage.
   For more details please see demos/dummyfunction_readme
   
  './multinumerics.pl [-d myworkingdirectory -p mymultiparamfilename]' or
   this is used to automate the calculation of many different numeric points.
   For more details please see demos/Readme_multinumerics


DEMOS: the 'demos' subdirectory contains parameter and template files for the examples 
              described in the manuscript. 
	      The file 'timings.info' gives a list of timings for the different examples 
	      (indicative timings only, as this is obviously machine dependent).
	      The results which a correct usage of the program should produce are 
	      also given in the 'demos' subdirectory,  in the files with extension *.res.


USEFUL FOR TROUBLESHOOTING:

-- If the numerical integration takes very long:
apart from the fact that this is to be expected for complicated integrands, other reasons could be

 - the integrand still contains undefined symbols at the numerical integration stage
because the numerical values for the constants have not been properly defined
(e.g. values for which the integrand has a singularity within the integration range).
 - When using divonne, 'border' is set too small. This is particularly important
 when the integrand ~1/sqrt(x) (1/sqrt(1-x)) when x->0 (x->1), or the integrand
 has a complicated pole structure.
Things to do are: 
check the log files of the numerical integration in the subdiretories 
 integralname/polestructure/epstothe[i], where ``polestructure" is of the form e.g.  2l0h0, 
denoting 2 logarithmic poles and 0 linear or higher poles.

- or you chose a very large number of Monte Carlo points or an extremely high accuracy 
in the input file


-- If the results do not appear in an editor window:
either you did not specify an editor in param.input (last entry) or 
your system is unable to open the editor window. 
In this case just look at the result file located in the  integralname} subdirectory 
(where  integralname is the name for the calculated integral or graph, 
specified by you  in param.input, third item). The result file is called integralname\_[point]full.res.

-- If you get the message ``path for Mathematica not automatically found":
Insert the path to Mathematica on your system manually for the variable  \mathpath
in the file  perlsrc/mathlaunch.pl.




